Stored Procedures
==================
-- A stored procedures is a named, precompiled collection of one or more SQL statements
that are stored inside the database and can be executed(called) whenever required.
Instead of sending a long block of SQL from an application every time, the application
simply calls the procedure by name

-- Stored Procedures behave likes functions or subroutines in programming language 
they can accept parameters, perform logic(loops, conditions, variables declarations)
and return results


Why use stored procedures?
---------------------------
1.Performance:-precompiled and cached by the database engine ,so repeated execution 
is faster then sending raw SQL each time
2.Reusability:-write the logic once,call it from many applications or scripts
3.Security:-User can be granteed execute permission on a procedure without granting 
direct access to the underlying tables
4.Reduced network traffic:- A single CALL statement replaces may lines of SQL sent over the network
5.Maintainability:- Business logic is centralized in the database , so changes are made in
one palce
6.Consistency:-Ensures the same validation/business rules are applied every time ,regradless of which application calls it

Advantages
-----------
*Faster ececution after complilation
*Reduce duplicate SQL
*Supports transactions,loops,conditions,variables,cursors and exception handlers
*Easy maintenance

Disadvantges
--------------
*Debugging can be difficult
*Vendor-specific syntax
*Complex procedyres may become hard to maintain 

Create Procedure
-----------------
syntax:

DELIMITER//

CREATE PROCEDURE PROCEDURE_NAME(
[IN|OUT|INOUT] PARAMETER-NAME DATA-TYE....)
BEGIN                                     
---- SQL Statements/logic
------Declarations,conditions,loops...etc
END//


DELIMITER;
CALL PROCEDURE_NAME();
